# Fields that could not be measured are OMITTED, never invented — no fabricated ORCID,
# DOI, arXiv id or paper title appears in this file. Works this repository cites in docs/
# by identifier only, without title or author metadata in repo text (Carles-Danchin-Saut
# 2012, Wallstrom 1994, Meng-Yang 2024, Khesin-Misiolek-Modin, the Khesin-Modin preprint,
# the Gross-Pitaevskii reconnection paper, Ding-Chung-Illingworth, Orszag dealiasing), are
# recorded as identifier-bearing entries in docs/CITATIONS.md instead of being invented here.
#
# `title` = the README's H1 ("# Navier") + the repository description measured from gh api.
# `license` = the GitHub-detected SPDX id. The license is MIXED and the repository records
# that itself: the adapted construction modules preserve upstream Apache-2.0 notices
# (docs/OPENAI_CONSTRUCTION_PROVENANCE.md) while original material is MIT
# (docs/PREPUBLICATION_REVIEW.md); "MIT AND Apache-2.0" is the measured-accurate alternative
# if the schema carrier lane prefers an SPDX expression over a single id.
# `year`/`date-released`: created_at == 2026-07-14T21:47:32Z with visibility == public, and
# the first tracked commit is 2026-07-14 09:58:52 -0700. gh api exposes no
# visibility-transition history, so the first-public year is INFERRED, not measured.
# `authors`: [A-CONFIRM] law item 3 forbids a bare org as sole author where persons
# authored. Measured basis (git-log author census on origin/main): 1246 "Chronos Bot",
# 8 "Tim Jacoby <timothyjacoby@gmail.com>", 2 "awkronos-bot". Bot identities carry no
# personal name, so Tim Jacoby is the only human identity in the repository's own metadata;
# confirm the attribution with Tim, and add `version:`/`commit-revision:` when a release
# tag exists. ORCID is omitted rather than guessed.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: If you use this repository, please cite it as below. The theorem-level, per-result citations
  (exact Lean declaration names, and what each one does and does not claim) are in docs/CITATIONS.md;
  the prior-work bibliography with identifiers is references/manifest.json.
title: 'Navier: Lean formalization of forced Navier–Stokes breakdown, unforced regularity frontiers,
  and a Rust spectral fluid solver'
type: software
authors:
- given-names: Tim
  family-names: Jacoby
- name: Awkronos
repository-code: https://github.com/awkronos/navier
abstract: A native Lean 4 reconstruction of the forced three-dimensional Navier–Stokes breakdown
  construction, with analytical continuation criteria and reproducible spectral fluid solvers.
  The checked theorem Navier.Breakdown.ConstructedBreakdown.wholeSpaceBreakdown inhabits the repository's
  whole-space alternative C, and the checked theorem PeriodicConstructedBreakdown.periodicBreakdown
  inhabits the periodic alternative D. The unforced global regularity statements A and B remain
  open in this repository; the solver fields and residuals are numerical evidence, not the continuum
  proof.
license: MIT
# measured 2026-11-03 against the CFF 1.2.0 schema itself (gh api
#   repos/citation-file-format/citation-file-format/contents/schema.json?ref=1.2.0,
#   root cff-version pattern ^1\.2\.0$): there is NO root-level 'year' key and no
#   'contributors' key; additionalProperties is false at root. The release date is therefore
#   carried only by 'date-released' below, and contributorship is carried by docs/CITATIONS.md.
# (1.2.0: no root year) year: 2026
# So the year question this draft flagged is settled by the schema, not by choice: the only
# date key 1.2.0 allows at root is date-released, which carries 2026-07-14 (created_at, and
# the date of the first tracked commit). If a later CFF version is ever targeted, re-measure
# that version's schema first - this lane looked for a 1.3.0 tag in the schema repository and
# got HTTP 404 ("No commit found for the ref 1.3.0"), so it asserts nothing about 1.3.x keys.
date-released: '2026-07-14'
